  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Cultivating Change - Bangladesh</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Though aiming to be democratic country, Bangladesh hasn&apos;t achieved the state of efficiency in the last 53 years, in fact, has rather seen rises and falls of dictatorships. The country achieved significant economic growth, but is bearing the aftermaths of turbulent political conditions that has translated into severe inequality, shrinking civic space, concentrated power, top -down non -inclusive policy, gender -based discrimination, etc. The climate change impacts exacerbate these challenges and hampers plans towards poverty alleviation, food security, and social well -being. Bangladesh is recognized as the 7th most climate -vulnerable, however, the most climate -vulnerable are the grower communities (e.g., farmers, fisherfolks, 41% of the workforce). As we reflect on the civic space trends and challenges, being 0% emitters, this community is deprived of their resource rights, access to climate finance, fair markets, and decisions. Among these communities, women are impacted differently. As of 2024, women make up approximately 58% of the agricultural workforce in Bangladesh, and are often the primary caregivers for livestock and poultry, working as unpaid family laborers or in informal sectors. However, the majority of them lack financial independence and control over the income they earn. Since they are excluded in decision making process across all levels, majority of them cannot access or avail policies that protect their rights.  For reasons stated above, Oxfam in Bangladesh Cultivating Change programme focuses on the building networks and allies for growing the civic space, through leadership trainings to women, youth and CSOs, on climatic and geopolitical contexts. The ultimate aim is to promote locally -led indigenous adaptation practices in agriculture that can access climate finance for the grower communities to become sustainable and resilient against climate change  Year one activities include consultations, assessments, grower knowledge hub formation, trainings,</narrative>
  </description>
